  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThe upper level disturbance has not moved much today and has kept \nconvection northeast of the area. We're still expecting it to start \ndrifting more southwest this afternoon though and will keep a 20-30% \nchance for showers storms in the Victoria Crossroads. Farther south \ndid add some low (20%) pops along the seabreeze considering the \nconvection that did develop yesterday. PWATs are a bit lower though, \nso it's not a certainty that we'll get any development this \nafternoon. As the mid-level energy and increased moisture move more \nover the region tomorrow and Sunday we should see an increase in \nconvection activity. We're still only looking at 20-30% for most of \nthe area with up to 50% in the Victoria Crossroads. Monday looks a \nbit quieter as this disturbance moves out of the region before \nanother wave moves into the area on Tuesday into Wednesday \nincreasing PoPs to 20-30 across the area. \n\nHigh temperatures remain above normal overall through the period, \nbut if/where showers/storms move through around peak heating time, \nsome these locations could be a bit below normal for any given day. \nThe additional sky cover and rain coverage should keep us just below \nHeat Advisory criteria through the weekend. However, the WBGT UIL \nrisk will remain in the moderate risk level with heat indices 105-\n110 and corresponding air temperatures from the mid 90s along the \ncoast to 100-105 over the Brush Country.",
